      "latency_ms": 9020.6,
      "prompt_tokens": 25,
      "completion_tokens": 1137,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance with the molecular formula **H₂O**, consisting of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. It is a vital component of life and one of the most abundant and unique substances on Earth. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical and Physical Properties**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water is a polar molecule, meaning it has a partial positive charge on the hydrogen atoms and a partial negative charge on the oxygen atom. This polarity enables hydrogen bonding between molecules, which influences many of its properties.\n- **States of Matter**: Water exists in three states: **solid** (ice), **liquid** (water), and **gas** (water vapor). It has a unique property of expanding when it freezes, making ice less dense than liquid water (which is why ice floats).\n- **Physical Traits**: It is colorless, odorless, and tasteless in its pure form. It has a high **specific heat capacity** (absorbs and releases heat slowly), which helps regulate temperature in ecosystems and living organisms.\n\n### **Unique Characteristics**\n- **Hydrogen Bonding**: The hydrogen bonds between water molecules give it high **cohesion** (molecules stick together) and **adhesion** (ability to stick to other surfaces), which is critical for processes like capillary action in plants.\n- **Solvent Properties**: Water is often called the \"universal solvent\" because it can dissolve more substances than any other liquid, facilitating chemical reactions in biological systems and environmental processes.\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Essential for Life**: All known forms of life depend on water. It serves as a medium for biochemical reactions, transports nutrients and waste in organisms, and maintains cell structure.\n- **Human Body**: Approximately **60% of the human body** is water, crucial for functions like temperature regulation, digestion, and circulation.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Water Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection, sustaining ecosystems and weather patterns.\n- **Distribution**: About **97% of Earth's water** is saltwater (oceans), while the remaining 3% is freshwater, mostly locked in ice or underground.\n\n### **Why It Matters**\nWater's unique properties—polarity, hydrogen bonding, and solvent capabilities—make it indispensable for life, climate regulation, and countless natural processes. Its ability to exist in multiple states and its role in the environment underscore its significance in both scientific and everyday contexts."
